" You Can See Everything " has surpassed $100 million globally, a rare box office milestone for a documentary.

So far, the zeitgeist-capturing look at Theranos fraudster Elizabeth Holmes has generated a staggering $93.4 million at the domestic box office and $9 million overseas for a worldwide tally of $102.4 million. It continues to mint money for A24 , which produced the film in secret for roughly $7 million. Related Stories How to Get Started on Making a Difference as Tax Rules for Charitable Giving Change The Business of Being a Business Manager; 'The Secret Reading Club of Kabul' Directors on Finding Inspiration and Courage

Over the weekend, "You Can See Everything" opened in the United Kingdom with $3.2 million from 596 locations. Those ticket sales register as A24's second-largest debut in the territory behind "Backrooms" ($5.7 million). Similar to in the United States, around 70% of the audience in the U.K. was under 35 years old. Australia and New Zealand is currently the top-grossing market with $3.8 million. blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-variety-article-mid-article-uid0' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["mid-article1","mid-articleX","mid","mid-article"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-article") .addSize([[300,250],[2,2],[300,251],[2,4],[4,2],[320,480],[620,366],[300,450]]) .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; });

Elsewhere, the film is getting a staggered rollout at the international box office: it's also playing in Turkey, Poland, Czech Republic, Russia, United Arab Emirates and Greece (contributing around $2 million in total), and it'll debut over the next few weeks in other territories including Mexico, Brazil and Argentina (Nov. 26 and 25) Germany, Austria and Hong Kong (Dec. 3 and 4), Spain (Dec. 25), France (Dec. 30) and Japan (March 12). A24 is handling the release in select territories, with Sony distributing in markets including Denmark, Norway, Sweden and the Middle East.

Already, "You Can See Everything" is one of the biggest theatrical documentaries in years. It'll rely on overseas audiences to topple the genre's all-time record. Michael Moore's seminal 2004 film &#x201C;Fahrenheit 9/11&#x201D; is the highest-grossing documentary in history with $222 million globally, including $119 million domestically and $103 million overseas; followed by &#x201C;March of the Penguins&#x201D; with $127 million globally, including $77 million domestically and $50 million overseas. blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-dsk-tab-mid-article2-uid1' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["mid","mid-article2","mid-articleX"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-article2") .addSize([[320,480],[300,250],[300,251],[2,4],[4,2],[620,366],[300,450]]) .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; });

Directed by comedian Nathan Fielder and filmmaker Lance Oppenheim, "You Can See Everything" became a word-of-mouth smash after a surprise world premiere at Telluride Film Festival. The movie opened to $15 million domestically in mid October and, with sensational reviews, remained a big-screen draw throughout the fall. The nearly three-hour-long doc takes place over three years and begins as Holmes, the founder of the health technology company Theranos, is preparing for her 11-year prison sentence for defrauding investors.

In previous eras of less pervasive social media, we usually could only guess at just how disappointed music and film personalities were when they didn't fare well with Grammy or Oscar nominations. But lately, more and more major and minor figures have been up-front and honest in sharing with their fans that, hey, yes, they're feeling the apparent lack of industry love, too. As long as these reflections come off as more bummed out and philosophical than bitterly entitled or peevish &#x2014; here's looking at you, John Summit &#x2014; they can make for a moment of connection between artists and their fan base. And so it is that, this past week, I followed one of our most likably candid superstars through what I came to refer to as the Five Stages of Noah Kahan Grammy Grief. blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-variety-article-mid-article-uid0' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["mid-article1","mid-articleX","mid","mid-article"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-article") .addSize([[300,250],[2,2],[300,251],[2,4],[4,2],[320,480],[620,366],[300,450]]) .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; });

Kahan himself skipped over the anger phase, though his fans certainly felt that one, when it turned out that their hero got passed over for everything but one relatively modest nom, in defiance of all expectation. There were other stages they went through together. First came uneasy anticipation: The day before nominations came out, Kahan issued a whatever-will-be-will-be-type tweet, almost as if he'd gotten a hint that things were not going to go well for him. ("Whatever happens tomorrow I love you all and I have done everything I&#x2019;ve ever needed to do in my career because of YOUR support.") Then came resignation and premature acceptance: "Life goes on." Then came gallows humor, as he took a fake phone call on stage from the Grammys and quipped, "Wrong number." And finally, there was real acceptance, as he admitted in an affecting stage monologue that he was having a teachable moment as, without mentioning the awards, Kahan said he was assessing why he had hoped too much for something and put the value of his worth in the wrong place. blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-dsk-tab-mid-article2-uid1' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["mid","mid-article2","mid-articleX"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-article2") .addSize([[320,480],[300,250],[300,251],[2,4],[4,2],[620,366],[300,450]]) .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; });

Undoubtedly, some would say he should have just put himself above it all and not even referenced the seeming snub, but my admiration is always going to go more toward people who address elephants in rooms. So maybe now he could move forward with his usual good humor… while, sure, having the fact that he just sold out his next 24 U.S. stadium shows to keep him warm at night.

But maybe us Grammy prognosticators aren't yet ready to move on, because like pollsters in 2024, we want to figure out how we got Kahan's chances wrong. And really, he was the only significant thing we forecasted incorrectly, since virtually everything else in the top three categories went as predicted, for once. Kahan seemed to have almost everything going for him, being the only artist who consistently had not just one but two albums in the top 10 all year long, on top of having recently become one of the biggest ticket-sellers in the business… and , lest the predictions be based only on popular appeal, he'd also gotten mostly great reviews lately, although he hadn't started out his career as a critical favorite.

Professional prognosticators nearly all had Kahan's "The Great Divide" album landing one of the eight open slots for album of the year, and most entertainment news outlets predicted the same for the title track in the fields of record and/or song of the year. The forecasters lining up to say he was close to a sure thing included those at Billboard, Rolling Stone, the Hollywood Reporter, Hits and, yes, Variety . Over at the awards site Gold Derby, "Great Divide" was seen as all but locked in for an album nomination no matter which of their columns you looked down &#x2014; industry experts, their editors, or the site's active followers. Instead, he wound up with a lone nod for… best contemporary folk recording, which was rather like the Recording Academy setting a bull down in a china shop instead of at the rodeo.

There was one obvious stumbling block on the path to considering him a shoo-in for one of more big nods: the fact that he'd only gotten two nominations before, and only one as the primary person nominated. Kahan was nominated as best new artist for the 2024 Grammys (and lost to the empirically less popular Victoria Monet); his only other prior nom was in a country category as the featured duet partner on a Kelsea Ballerini single. But surely we were past the point now &#x2014; we thought &#x2014; where anyone still had to dude-splain Noah Kahan to the Grammy elite. blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-variety-article-mid-articleX-uid2' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["btf","mid-articleX","mid"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-articleX") .addSize([[320,480],[300,250],[300,251],[620,366]]) .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; });

Of course it shouldn't matter if "The Great Divide" spent most of 2026 in the top 10 hovering just above the continued presence of the four-year-old (!) "Stick Season." if these albums blew chunks… which is the kind of thing that's been known to sink a Grammy nomination. But "The Great Divide" demonstrably does not fall into the realm of suckage. If anything, it represents the kind of perfect storm the Grammys have traditionally loved to reward, where somehow, some way, songwriting that is idiosyncratic and qualitatively strong and unique captures the imagination of the masses who might usually be stereotyped as tending toward less distinctly personal fare. Kahan could have been seen as having it all…

Except for having a natural constituency among Recording Academy members.

Therein lies a rub. It did nag at me, even as I was making my own forecast that he'd get into the top categories, that I couldn't picture in my mind's eye which subset of voters would be putting their support behind him. In terms of either genre or geography, he did not and does not have the wind at his back.

The Recording Academy has 12 chapters spread across the U.S. This may come as a shock, but none of them are in Burlington. There's a Pacific Northwest chapter, but good luck looking for a corresponding Atlantic Northeast chapter. Surely Kahan would be the first to laugh at the idea of his New England industry homies colluding as a voting bloc to ensure vindication for Vermont.

The genre thing looms as an even bigger problem. Leaving aside the general field at the top of the ballot, where does a Kahan rightfully belong among the 90+ stylistically divided categories that follow? You could consider him rock. Billboard certainly does; he has two songs in the top 10 of the rock airplay chart right now ("Orbiter" and "Draw You Out"). But are the voters who choose to vote in the rock categories &#x2014; and as you probably know, they don't get to vote in all 100 &#x2014; really going there to vote for a guy like Kahan, as opposed to the harder stuff? He would seem like a closer fit for the Americana and American roots categories. But the Americana community hasn't really fully embraced him, even though he has shown up to their events. He didn't come up through their farm system, and in a genre that deliberately favors underdogs, he might've simply gotten too popular too fast. So, "contemporary folk recording" it is, even if that is one oddball catch-all category. (But as has been pointed out, there are people nominated in that every year who have the nom as a highlight of their professional careers, so there should be no shame in it for Kahan, and he'd undoubtedly agree.) blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-variety-article-mid-articleX-uid3' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["btf","mid-articleX","mid"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-articleX") .addSize([[320,480],[300,250],[300,251],[620,366]]) .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; });

If you really want to know why we all should have seen Kahan's near-shutout coming, anyway, consider the ongoing case of Zach Bryan, who also falls between genres, despite also being one of the biggest recording and touring artists in the universe at the moment. I thought of Bryan as I was wondering whether Kahan would face the same problem with the Grammys, but at the time, I thought Bryan might have been snubbed most of all because of his surlier personality, whereas Kahan being an overtly personable guy would make the difference. Turns out they're probably just in the same ballpark after all.

And then there is the issue of white guys with guitars, and how they're doing, generally, in the culture, or with the Grammys specifically. The demographics the Recording Academy has been courting in expanding and diversifying its membership do not suggest they're bringing in vast amounts of people who are going to put a Kahan at the top of their ballot, any more than they're going to vote for Paul McCartney (whose critically hailed "Boys of Dungeon Lane" would have been a shoo-in for top noms with the Grammy voters of the 2000s, but not the 2020s). When the Grammys invited 3,600 pros to join as voting members in the class of 2025, 56% were people of color and 60% were under 40. You don't look at those stats and imagine that any of the various waning subgenres of rock are suddenly going to be overrepresented among the biggest Grammy nominees.

But here's the other thing to take into account: all those new voters, along with the older ones the Academy has not aged it out, are getting it right more often than not. As much as I like "The Great Divide," when I look at the album of the year lineup to see which nominee I would personally knock out to make room for it, there are only a couple of the eight that seem even remotely expendable. Most forecasters would probably say that Phoebe Bridgers' "Lost Weekend" took a slot that was expected to go to Kahan, and maybe she knocked him down to ninth place, although we'll never know for sure where he ended up. There's absolutely nothing regrettable about her making it in, or the utterly deserving Rosal&#xED;a or Raye or most of the others… and obviously no shame in women earning seven out of eight noms in some of the top categories, after the "step up, girls" era, and on merit. Of all the years for any sense of overriding Grammy outrage to take hold, this is not one of them. blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-variety-article-mid-articleX-uid4' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["btf","mid-articleX","mid"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-articleX") .addSize([[320,480],[300,250],[300,251],[620,366]]) .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; });

But I can think of other artists besides Kahan I think got "robbed" in the top categories, like Sienna Spiro, who should have been up for all four top general-field trophies but is only in for best new artist. A year this solid and action-packed makes the case that the Academy should considering reupping the number of finalists in the top four categories from eight to 10, as was the case in 2022 and 2023 before tjeu dropped it back down again. If the Oscars can stick with 10 nominees at the top of the ballot, so could the Grammys.

In the meantime, as Kahan fans lick wounds, they ought to be able to take some comfort in the hundred factors that make even a worthy contender get shut out, or nearly ignored. It's not him, it's them &#x2014; in all sorts of ways &#x2014; and there are real reasons to believe that, not just the delusional ones hardcore stans come up with to justify more negligible artists being slighted. And if the reasons for being left out have to do with being the kind of artist who snuck up completely between the cracks, and who's thriving beyond belief, even as he continues to be a little bit stepped over, that's a triumph that might finally be even better than a Grammy.

The odds were not in Noah Schnapp &#x2019;s favor when it came to joining &#x201C;The Hunger Games: Sunrise on the Reaping .&#x201D;

The &#x201C; Stranger Things &#x201D; actor revealed on social media on Saturday that he had auditioned for the lead role of Haymitch. &#x201C;POV: my audition for Haymitch,&#x201D; he wrote on his Instagram story. Tagging leading man Joseph Zada, Schnapp added, &#x201C;Think they picked right for this one.&#x201D; Related Stories Hollywood Has Flipped on GenAI, Says 'Avatar' VFX House Weta FX Chief: 'If You Don’t Have an AI Strategy, Don’t Bother'

The prequel "Hunger Games" film follows Zada as Haymitch through his tumultuous experience during the 50th annual Hunger Games and the beginning of the revolution. The fifth installment of the franchise is based on Suzanne Collins&#x2019; 2025 novel of the same name. It comes three years after the franchise's first prequel, &#x201C;The Ballad of Songbirds and Snakes." Zada is joined by a star-studded cast including McKenna Grace , Jesse Plemons, Kieran Culkin , Maya Hawke and more. blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-variety-article-mid-article-uid0' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["mid-article1","mid-articleX","mid","mid-article"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-article") .addSize([[300,250],[2,2],[300,251],[2,4],[4,2],[320,480],[620,366],[300,450]]) .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; });

Schnapp may be onto something with his casting assessment, given the prequel&#x2019;s massive box-office success over opening weekend. The highly anticipated film opened to $120 million domestically and earned $105 million internationally for a global tally of $225 million.

While he may have been denied the coveted role in &#x201C;Sunrise on the Reaping,&#x201D; Schnapp is still booked and busy. The 22-year-old actor is currently starring in Andy Sandberg&#x2019;s play &#x201C;Affluenza,&#x201D; marking his professional stage debut at Riverside Studios in London. The dark comedy, inspired by real-life events, follows 16-year-old Dylan (Schnapp) dealing with the aftermath of causing a fatal drunk-driving crash in the Hamptons. Alongside Schnapp, the cast includes Janette Manrara, Juliet Cowan, Wallis Currie-Wood and Rupert Young.

Tom Jackson , former Denver Broncos linebacker and a Hall of Fame ESPN analyst, died Friday. He was 75.

Jackson&#x2019;s death was announced by ESPN, who was informed of his death by his family. ESPN &#x201C;SportsCenter&#x201D; anchor Chris Berman told the network that his death was &#x201C;unexpected,&#x201D; and he&#x2019;d spoken with Jackson &#x201C;at length&#x201D; on Tuesday. "He was a vital 75," Berman said. "Tom was poignant. He was active. This is a shock." Related Stories From a Mockbuster Version of 'The Odyssey' to a 'Jiu-Jitsu Nun': How Producers Are Keeping the Low-Budget Film Market Alive Via Streaming (and Even Lower Budgets) Steven Spielberg Calls ‘The Odyssey’ a ‘Showstopper’ and ‘One of the Greatest Things’ Matt Damon Has Ever Done

In a statement released by ESPN, Berman said &#x201C;all of us are stunned.&#x201D; &#x201C;I'm stunned, we're stunned and you're stunned. We've lost one of the best human beings ever to walk the planet, Tom Jackson. I was fortunate to work side by side with Tom for 30 years, primarily on &#x2018;NFL Primetime&#x2019; and &#x2018;Sunday NFL Countdown.&#x2019; It was a labor of love for our whole team, both in front of the camera and behind it. We always hoped our viewers enjoyed the shows as much as we did bringing it to them every week.&#x201D; blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-variety-article-mid-article-uid0' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["mid-article1","mid-articleX","mid","mid-article"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-article") .addSize([[300,250],[2,2],[300,251],[2,4],[4,2],[320,480],[620,366],[300,450]]) .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; });

Born on April 4, 1951, in Cleveland, Ohio, Jackson began his NFL career with the Denver Broncos in 1973 after a successful college football career at the University of Lousville. Jackson played in 191 regular-season games, including 177 starts and 10 playoff games over 14 seasons with the team. Jackson was a starter on the Broncos' 1977 Super Bowl team; a three-time Pro Bowl selection and a first-team All Pro in 1977 and 1978; and among the team&#x2019;s top five tacklers in 11 consecutive seasons.

"The Denver Broncos are heartbroken by the passing of Ring of Fame linebacker Tom Jackson, a cornerstone of the legendary Orange Crush defense and one of the most beloved players in franchise history," the Broncos said in a statement Sunday. "An inspirational leader and fierce competitor, TJ was a proud member of the Broncos' first Super Bowl team that helped establish a winning tradition in Denver. Off the field, Tom carried himself with class, warmth and humility, forging deep connections and lifelong friendships with teammates and throughout Broncos Country. blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-dsk-tab-mid-article2-uid1' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["mid","mid-article2","mid-articleX"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-article2") .addSize([[320,480],[300,250],[300,251],[2,4],[4,2],[620,366],[300,450]]) .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; });

After stepping away from the field, Jackson had an illustrious 29-year career at ESPN as an analyst alongside Berman. (ESPN's NFL studio in Bristol, Connecticut, is named the Chris Berman-Tom Jackson Studio, after the two.) He retired in 2016, a year after being awarded the Pete Rozelle Radio-Television award by the Hall of Fame in 2015.

Jackson was "one of the most influential and beloved figures in ESPN's history,&#x201D; ESPN chairman Jimmy Pitaro said in a statement released by the network. "He brought the credibility of an All-Pro NFL player, the insight of a gifted analyst and a warmth and authenticity that connected with generations of football fans," Pitaro said in a statement. "Alongside Chris Berman, he helped make NFL Primetime an iconic part of our football weekends. In fact, ESPN's NFL studio is named the Chris Berman-Tom Jackson Studio, a distinction unmatched in our history and a fitting tribute to their lasting impact.&#x201D;

He continued: "Away from the set, Tom was a trusted colleague, generous mentor and dear friend to many. We extend our deepest condolences to his family, friends, former teammates and all who loved him. His legacy at ESPN, throughout the NFL and among the countless fans who admired his incredible work will endure."

Jackson is survived by his wife, Jennifer, and his two daughters, Taylor and Morgan.

I meet Oobah Butler on a wet afternoon in Chinatown outside an empty claw-machine arcade.

I spot the 34-year-old British hoax artist and gonzo journalist from way down the street, his platinum-blond mop top bobbing between umbrella-wielding tourists.

The reason he picked this interview location &#x2014; a few blocks from the open-air counterfeit handbag hub of Canal Street &#x2014; becomes clear when he pulls out his phone. Giddily, he shows me a TikTok page called Clawmaster, an account dedicated to teaching followers how to win prizes out of these notoriously tricky arcade machines. blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-variety-article-mid-article-uid0' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["mid-article1","mid-articleX","mid","mid-article"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-article") .addSize([[300,250],[2,2],[300,251],[2,4],[4,2],[320,480],[620,366],[300,450]]) .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; }); Related Stories Former Bond Villain Rami Malek Says Robert Pattinson Will Be an ‘Extraordinary’ 007: ‘I Could Not Be More Proud’ Taylor Swift, Jaafar Jackson, ‘The Odyssey’ Among Hollywood Music in Media Awards Winners

&#x201C;Let&#x2019;s out-scam the scammers,&#x201D; he says, grinning like the Cheshire Cat. We buy 50 tokens, and, as a bonus, the cashier lets Butler select from a mysterious deck of cards. Butler pulls from the top, revealing a Joker emblazoned with an anime character. She tells him he can redeem it later, so he slips it in his pocket. Butler sets his sights on a Charizard plushie, and we get to work.

Scamming is Butler&#x2019;s strong suit. Over the last decade, he&#x2019;s gone viral a dozen or so times, pulling off stunts like listing bottles of Amazon drivers&#x2019; urine as an energy drink on the shopping platform and creating the Shed at Dulwich, an entirely fake restaurant that climbed to No. 1 in London on TripAdvisor. During a television press tour for the Shed, Butler auditioned lookalikes to do interviews in his place, fooling journalists around the world.

Now, Butler is preparing to reveal five more fraudulent feats on his new HBO show, &#x201C;Oobah: Scams for a Better Tomorrow,&#x201D; which debuts Sunday, Nov. 22. In the series, Butler immerses himself in the worlds of wellness, wine, music and online dating, and scams his way to the top. With a style somewhere between Nathan Fielder and Sacha Baron Cohen, Butler hilariously exposes the bullshit at the heart of our culture. blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-dsk-tab-mid-article2-uid1' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["mid","mid-article2","mid-articleX"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-article2") .addSize([[320,480],[300,250],[300,251],[2,4],[4,2],[620,366],[300,450]]) .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; });

An example: In the second episode, Butler takes smoke-tainted wine, which is considered so unsellable that vineyards distill it into hand sanitizer, and through clever manipulation and questionable marketing, earns endorsements from two of the world&#x2019;s top sommeliers and the New York Times wine critic. By the end of the episode, he&#x2019;s selling it for $500 per bottle.

This penchant for scheming is in Butler&#x2019;s blood.

&#x201C;My great-grandfather came to America in the late 19th century and was in the fur trade. It says in our family history that he used to sell rabbit droppings rolled in flour as medicine to people,&#x201D; he says. &#x201C;So I come from a long line of bullshit artists &#x2014; or rabbit shit artists.&#x201D;

By this point, Butler and I have sunk at least 30 tokens into the claw machines with nothing to show for it. Marios, Pok&#xE9;mon and blue aliens in Italian soccer jerseys continue to slip our metal grasp, and we&#x2019;re starting to doubt the Clawmaster&#x2019;s expertise. Who is scamming whom?

&#x201C;He suggested that if you drop the claw right down into the hole, it loosens up,&#x201D; Butler says. But none of the TikTok tips are working. See, Clawmaster never posts footage of him losing. And, also, he only works on machines where the claw dangles from an elastic cord; the machines here at Anime Claw are much more rigid, dropping down from a taut wire. &#x201C;For God&#x2019;s sake, this is infuriating!&#x201D; Butler yells as another Charizard falls back into the pile. &#x201C;Clawmaster fucked us.&#x201D;

Aside from a finale stunt six years in the making, Butler filmed nearly all of &#x201C;Scams for a Better Tomorrow&#x201D; in the last few months. He initially pitched the last episode &#x2014; which is far too ambitious and surprising to spoil here &#x2014; to A24 as a feature documentary, but the studio passed. Later, its television department reached out to Butler and said, &#x201C;We want to make this a show.&#x201D; With the help of Josh Safdie&#x2019;s Central Pictures and &#x201C;Nathan for You&#x201D; production house Abso Lutely, Butler landed a pilot order at HBO, which would turn into a full series.

The first episode sees Butler breaking into the wellness industry with a skin serum made of children&#x2019;s tears. The influencers love it; Wall Street wants to throw money at it. A real New York dermatologist, after witnessing a group of kids crying into tubes, offers to endorse it &#x2014; in exchange for a fee, of course. blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-variety-article-mid-articleX-uid2' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["btf","mid-articleX","mid"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-articleX") .addSize([[320,480],[300,250],[300,251],[620,366]]) .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; });

